  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 144 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nMain focus after Columbus Day will be on rain chances mid-week and a \ntransition to more seasonable temps next weekend and perhaps \nsome frost/freeze potential.\n\nMonday-Tuesday Night...a weak cold front to move through early \nTuesday, with the NBM showing some slight chance PoPs across \nnorthwest IL. However, moisture availability will be limited and it \nmay end up being just a dry fropa. A 1023 mb sfc high to build into \nthe upper Midwest in the wake of the front bringing dry and pleasant \nconditions for the rest of Tuesday and at least part of Wednesday. \nDespite chance PoPs in the forecast, I wouldn't be surprised if \neasterly low level flow keeps the daylight hours Wednesday dry for \nmuch of the CWA. There is also some model discrepancies in \navailible moisture, strength of WAA, and forcing along an 850mb \ntheta-e gradient. \n\nWednesday night-Thursday...a weak upper level wave is progged to \nlift northeast out of the Plains ahead of western CONUS low and \nperhaps including some remnant moisture from Hurricane Simon. Where \nthis wave goes will largely be dependent on strength of the \nupper high centered over the lower Mississippi River valley. In \nany case, this period to bring high chance/low likely PoPs \n(40-60%) for scattered showers and isolated storms. While \nNBM/ENS/GEFS 24hr probabilities for >0.10\" QPF ending 00z Fri \nhave decreased, they are still in the 55-80% range (highest \nvalues on the GEFS and south of I-80). Thursday highs will be \ndependent on cloud cover/precip but are expected to be closer to\nseasonable numbers, with highs in the mid 60s/low 70s. \n\nFriday-Next Weekend...cooler and more seasonable conditions are \nforecast as a cold front moves through the area. As expected at this \ntime range, there is considerable differences in deterministic and \nensemble solutions with regards to evolution of upper level \nfeatures. The GFS/GEFS want to eject the western CONUS upper low \nfaster than the ECMWF/ENS and phase it with another stronger upper \nlow moving east over Canada. The result would bring a colder \nairmass into the upper Midwest leading to a frost/freeze \npotential over the weekend. The AIGEFS and ECMWF AFIS solutions \nare not as cold, but would be closer to the GFS/GEFS and will \nbe something to watch in later runs. The 00z LREF 100 member \nensemble has 56% of its members, with a low temp of 40 degrees \nor lower for DBQ on Saturday.\n\nLooking further ahead, ensemble data suggests largely a dry pattern \ntaking shape for the middle of October with very low probabilities \nof QPF >0.10\". The latest CPC Day 8-14 day precipitation outlook \nshows greater than 75% combined probability of near/below normal \nprecipitation for the Oct 17th-23rd time frame.",
